#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087435 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f6ae25fc-16eb-598b-a288-742e4b46cb12" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Dimensions of a Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "6587b5f1-8a44-556a-a95e-20c54dfebc04" :question/math-academy-id "e-4367" :question/problem "What are the dimensions of the matrix ${A}$, where\n$\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 5 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 7\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "The matrix ${A}$ has $4$ rows and $3$ columns. Therefore, its dimensions are $4\\times 3$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087436 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "34fd903a-fa34-57b0-a098-08fa1cbd4f95" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Dimensions of a Matrix with One Row or Column"}] :question/id #uuid "74a9046e-e0a3-59d6-8b13-437e9a0cf04a" :question/math-academy-id "e-4370" :question/problem "What are the dimensions of the matrix ${D}$, where\n$\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "The matrix ${D}$ has $1$ row and $4$ columns. So, its dimensions are $1\\times 4$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087446 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed7286fe-b6e2-5544-8bc6-86afc6cdb813" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Matrix by Its Description"}] :question/id #uuid "1fcce090-8441-5547-ab52-18b0ff41ac5b" :question/math-academy-id "e-5017" :question/problem "Which of the following is a $3 \\times 2$ matrix?\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1.1 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "- The matrix $A$ has $2$ rows and $3$ columns. So, its dimensions are $2 \\times 3$. $\\quad{\\color{red}\\times}$\n- The matrix $B$ has $3$ rows and $2$ columns. So, its dimensions are $3 \\times 2$. $\\quad{\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n- The matrix $C$ has $1$ rows and $3$ columns. So, its dimensions are $1 \\times 3$. $\\quad{\\color{red}\\times}$\n\nTherefore, only $B$ is a $3 \\times 2$ matrix."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087591 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "10ca3efc-7d23-5bc1-ab41-f785bed69986" :knowledge-point/title "Matrices for Route Planning"}] :question/id #uuid "5c3b6d53-3dbb-59f6-8d15-a9e6f48927a1" :question/math-academy-id "e-9942" :question/problem "The diagram above shows train routes between three train stations, where the nodes represent stations and arrows represent the directed routes between them. The (incomplete) route matrix below represents the number of routes between the three stations. Find the value of $a+b+c$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/861/Source/Images/827e10f7394aae17cb1f29997709e039.png)\n\n|  |  | To: |  |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  |  | **A** | **B** | **C** |\n| From: | **A** | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}a}$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n| **B** | $0$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}b}$ | $2$ |  |\n| **C** | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}c}$ | $0$ | $1$ |  |" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our diagram.\n\n- There is $1$ arrow starting at Station A and ending at Station A. So, there is only one route from $A$ to $A$, and so $a=1$.\n- No arrows are starting at Station B and ending at Station B. So, there are no routes from $B$ to $B$, and so $b=0$.\n- There is $1$ arrow starting at Station C and ending at Station A. So, there is only one route from $C$ to $A$, and so $c=1$.\n\nAs a result, our route matrix is the following:\n\n|  |  | To: |  |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  |  | **A** | **B** | **C** |\n| From: | **A** | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1}$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n| **B** | $0$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}$ | $2$ |  |\n| **C** | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1}$ | $0$ | $1$ |  |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\na+b+c = 1+0+1 = 2\n$$"}]}
